<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch08_P04_Contrapositive_inside</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here is the proof above in numbered lines.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Suppose that \(a^2\) is even and \(a\) is not even.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Then \(a\) is odd, so \(a = 2c + 1\) for some integer \(c\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Then \(a^2 = (2c + 1)^2 = 4c^2 + 4c + 1 = 2(2c^2 + 2c) + 1\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>So \(a^2\) is odd.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>So \(a^2\) is even, by line 1, and \(a^2\) is odd, by line 4. This is a contradiction.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>So the assumption in line 1 is false: if \(a^2\) is even, then \(a\) is even.</td></tr>
</table>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Line 1 assumes two things: that \(a^2\) is even, and that \(a\) is odd. Apart from line 1, in which line is the assumption that \(a^2\) is even used?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Lines 2 to 4 do not use the assumption that \(a^2\) is even. Which statement do lines 2 to 4 prove on their own?</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>The assumption that \(a^2\) is even is used only in line 5, where it clashes with line 4. Lines 2 to 4 start from ‘\(a\) is odd’ and never mention that \(a^2\) is even.</p></li>
<li><p>Lines 2 to 4 prove, on their own, that if \(a\) is odd then \(a^2\) is odd. This is the <em>contrapositive</em> of the statement ‘if \(a^2\) is even, then \(a\) is even’: the contrapositive of ‘if P, then Q’ is ‘if not Q, then not P’, and a statement and its contrapositive are either both true or both false.</p>
<p>So the proof can be rewritten without a contradiction: ‘Suppose that \(a\) is odd. Then \(a = 2c + 1\) for some integer \(c\), and \(a^2 = 2(2c^2 + 2c) + 1\) is odd. So if \(a\) is odd, then \(a^2\) is odd; equivalently, if \(a^2\) is even, then \(a\) is even.’ Both versions are correct proofs.</p>
<p>‘If \(a\) is even, then \(a^2\) is even’ is the converse of the statement, and ‘if \(a^2\) is odd, then \(a\) is odd’ is the converse of the contrapositive. Both are true, but neither is what lines 2 to 4 prove.</p></li>
</ol>]]></text></generalfeedback>
